t being  actively followed as context when  they're diagnosed and again while the  numbers of those are very very small  that is is not a good parameter and  again in terms of that's a community  level where we've seen that's in the  community again they all represents  opportunities for disease transmission  at the moment as of July the 11th we're  tracking 5580 contacts across very very  large and difficult geographic zone with  extreme logistics we've managed on a  daily basis to follow 4970 7 or 89%  nearly 90% of those contacts are  followed on a daily basis which is good  performance in the context of the the  extreme logistics in the area but it  also means that 10% of contacts are not  followed and that in itself is a concern  the in terms of community alerts we're  investigating or detecting about between  five and six hundred alerts at community  level every day of them 25 percent are  validated the suspect cases and and then  then those individuals are tested there  are still challenges in  testing we've very recently established  testing capacity in Bakura which is to  the south so we now have testing  capacities and in all of the effective  health zones and we have clinically  capacity with partners and all of the  affected households  you  same context as North kiru we don't have  the same necessarily the same security  issues but we do have very very big  logistics and distance and living  conditions  conditions were for staff and the  condition of the roads etc we've  vaccinated thousands of people with our  vaccination teams and we're very lucky  to have that  and those vaccination teams in place and  equally so therapeutics are in the field  and now being actively used to treat  cases the r0 for the last two weeks of  June was 1.7 which means technically  each case is generating more than one  other case so this is still an active  very active outbreak and I would say it  is still a great concern the ecuador  province is on on the river congo  it i